nina (Docs): Morning team  I'm opening a refresh PR for the quickstart that demonstrates a tiny hello-stream-app (SSE -> WebSocket examples), plus a short section on local auth-proxy for ephemeral keys. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/12678

jamal (Eng): Quick heads-up: runtime now prefers `/v1/responses/stream` for SSE and `/v1/responses/ws` for WebSocket upgrades. Also new header `x-rw-client-ver` is recommended for telemetry. Update snippets accordingly.

rachel (DevEx): Good call. For the auth-proxy, are we showing a one-file Node proxy or suggesting using the CLI `rw auth login --ephemeral`? I can add both but keep the proxy tiny.

nina (Docs): Let's include the CLI one-liner and a 12-line Node proxy example. Keep it optional. Add a short security blurb: never commit ephemeral tokens.

oscar (PM): Add a note under "First deploy" that Dedicated deployments require different hostnames for WS (ws://dedicated-<id>.redwood.net) and to contact sales for firewall rules.

jamal (Eng): Also, the TS SDK v9 changed streaming API from `client.streamResponses()` to `client.responses.stream()`. Please update code blocks.

rachel (DevEx): I'll update JS/TS samples. Draft snippet for the auth-proxy to paste into the quickstart README:

```js
// simple local auth proxy for ephemeral tokens
const http = require('http');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  const upstream = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_EPHEMERAL_TOKEN}`
    },
    body: await new Promise(r => {
      let b=''; req.on('data',c=>b+=c); req.on('end',()=>r(b));
    })
  });
  const text = await upstream.text();
  res.writeHead(upstream.status, {'Content-Type': 'application/json'});
  res.end(text);
}).listen(PORT, ()=>console.log('auth-proxy running', PORT));
```

nina (Docs): Perfect. For the WebSocket sample, prefer browser native WebSocket over fancy libs. Include reconnection tip.

jamal (Eng): Also update the sample repo Dockerfile base to `node:20-alpine` while you're at it. Node 18 is fine but a lot of folks are on 20 now. CI will need to be green.

